WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:07.900
Your body already holds cells that can bind viruses you have never met.

2
00:00:09.200 --> 00:00:11.460
How could it prepare for something it has never seen?

3
00:00:12.533 --> 00:00:14.273
There are two possible answers.

4
00:00:15.613 --> 00:00:20.453
A soft, general receptor might fold itself around each new antigen, like clay.

5
00:00:21.733 --> 00:00:25.553
Or receptors could be made first, and the antigen picks one that fits.

6
00:00:26.667 --> 00:00:30.947
Adaptive immunity is carried by lymphocytes, a kind of white blood cell.

7
00:00:32.227 --> 00:00:36.007
The molecule a lymphocyte's receptor binds is called an antigen.

8
00:00:37.150 --> 00:00:41.450
Macfarlane Burnet proposed the second answer, called clonal selection.

9
00:00:42.890 --> 00:00:46.050
Nearly every lymphocyte makes receptors of one shape.

10
00:00:47.230 --> 00:00:49.850
It makes them before it meets the antigen they fit.

11
00:00:50.933 --> 00:00:56.093
But the human genome holds far fewer protein-coding genes than there are receptor

12
00:00:56.093 --> 00:00:59.693
shapes. So how can it make millions of them?

13
00:01:00.683 --> 00:01:06.103
For a worked example, follow one developing B cell as it builds the heavy chain of

14
00:01:06.103 --> 00:01:11.103
its antibody. The tip of that chain is not encoded as one piece.

15
00:01:12.383 --> 00:01:15.963
It is built from short stretches of DNA, called gene segments.

16
00:01:17.243 --> 00:01:21.703
Here they sit in three rows: V segments, D segments and J segments.

17
00:01:22.767 --> 00:01:29.887
The cell picks one V, one D and one J. Enzymes cut the DNA, and the cell joins the

18
00:01:29.887 --> 00:01:31.047
three chosen pieces.

19
00:01:32.467 --> 00:01:35.427
That joined piece encodes the tip of one heavy chain.

20
00:01:36.500 --> 00:01:39.580
The screen shows illustrative human counts for each row.

21
00:01:40.880 --> 00:01:42.920
The cell takes one segment from each row.

22
00:01:44.160 --> 00:01:45.960
Do the choices add up, or multiply?

23
00:01:52.533 --> 00:02:01.093
They multiply. Every V can go with every D, and every such pair with every J. So 69

24
00:02:01.093 --> 00:02:06.933
segments give 5,520 V-D-J combinations for the heavy chain's variable region.

25
00:02:08.017 --> 00:02:09.697
And that is only one chain.

26
00:02:11.177 --> 00:02:13.217
Light chains use just V and J segments.

27
00:02:14.697 --> 00:02:18.537
T cells build their own receptor chains from segments of their own.

28
00:02:19.757 --> 00:02:23.617
Imprecise joins and chain pairing multiply the variety further.

29
00:02:24.850 --> 00:02:29.290
So the body holds a vast crowd of lymphocytes, each with its own receptor.

30
00:02:30.570 --> 00:02:35.150
It is like a shoe shop with millions of pairs already on the shelves, waiting for

31
00:02:35.150 --> 00:02:36.150
the right customer.

32
00:02:37.983 --> 00:02:39.543
Now an antigen arrives.

33
00:02:40.843 --> 00:02:44.983
It drifts past B cell after B cell, and most receptors ignore it.

34
00:02:46.083 --> 00:02:49.183
It binds only the few whose receptors already fit.

35
00:02:50.663 --> 00:02:55.043
T cells need the antigen shown to them on another cell, as the text explains.

36
00:02:56.217 --> 00:02:57.597
Those cells divide.

37
00:02:59.057 --> 00:03:04.177
Their descendants form a clone, a population descended from one cell, all starting

38
00:03:04.177 --> 00:03:05.197
with the same receptor.

39
00:03:06.400 --> 00:03:09.020
The antigen did not mold that receptor.

40
00:03:10.260 --> 00:03:12.600
It existed before the antigen arrived.

41
00:03:13.780 --> 00:03:16.080
The antigen chooses; it does not shape.
